Description: antinomie: observe io_uring and AIO requests
 A program can do its I/O without a system call per operation: through
 an io_uring ring it fills itself, or through Linux AIO. The work then
 happens later, often on another thread, and the system call that
 started it shows little of it. Record each request as it moves through
 the kernel.
 .
 A request gets an id and a series of records published under its
 submitter's identity: the submission (the SQE or iocb as the kernel
 copied it), acceptance or rejection, its input payload at issue, its
 output payload and result at completion, cancellation, and the final
 record. Typed operands describe what an opcode points to (paths,
 socket addresses, epoll events, timeouts, futex waits, provided
 buffers, file table updates, socket commands); a command it does not
 know is an explicit no-decoder gap. Payloads are read in the
 submitter's address space by whichever task runs the request (an io-wq
 worker or the SQPOLL thread shares it). An AIO read that completes in
 interrupt context, where its buffers cannot be read, is held in a
 64-entry reap table and its output is read when io_getevents() hands
 the event back, from a copy of the iovec array the kernel imported.
 Submissions from the SQPOLL thread or from outside the ring creator's
 thread group are attributed to the creator, and an entry only one
 process can have written is marked as written by that process.
 .
 Records are staged in a queue and published by a worker that leaves
 room in the CPU's ring for system call events (half of it, or an eighth
 when the host asks senders to wait); a task that may sleep waits for
 room in the queue instead of losing its record. The run counts requests
 in flight and their losses, and RUN_STATUS reports ASYNC_COMPLETE only
 when every request was observed. async_capture.c decodes the arguments
 of io_uring_setup, io_uring_enter, io_uring_register and the AIO system
 calls; the recorder learns to publish a record on behalf of a request's
 origin, with that task's identity and sequence counters.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - io_uring/io_uring.c: ring creation and teardown, submission,
   completion, cancellation and request free, where a request's life
   begins and ends.
 - io_uring/io_uring.h holds the helpers the opcode files use, and
   io_uring/{epoll,fs,futex,kbuf,net,openclose,rsrc,rw,statx,timeout,
   uring_cmd,waitid,xattr}.c describe their operands and payloads in
   their prep and issue functions, the only places that know them.
 - include/linux/io_uring_types.h adds the request's state at the end
   of struct io_kiocb (cmd_to_io_kiocb() needs ->cmd at offset 0, which
   a BUILD_BUG_ON keeps) and the ring creator to struct io_ring_ctx.
 - fs/aio.c: submission, completion under the completion lock (with the
   ring slot it used), the reap in io_getevents(), and the context's
   and the address space's teardown.
 - include/linux/sched.h and kernel/fork.c give each task event and
   packet counters, so its own events and the records published for its
   requests share one sequence.
 .
 Contract: async records are part of application protocol 1. The
 attribution flag PRODUCER_PROCESS and the async payload rules are
 additive within revision 17.
